C o m p i l a t i o n


      CEE 6410 - Water Resources Systems Analysis
      Example Coups Minivans from Lecture Example 2 on Graphical solution to LP
       
      A manufacturer can produce:  coups and minivans.  Data are as fol-lows:
       
      Seasonal Resource
      Inputs or Profit        Crops        Resource
      Availability
              Eggplant        Tomatoes
      Water        1x103 gal/plant        2x103 gal/plant      4x106 gal/year
      Land        4 ft2/plant        3 ft2/plant               1.2x104 ft2
      Labor         5hr/plant        2.5/hr plant              17,500 hours
      Profit/plant        $6        $7
       
                      Determine the optimal planting for the two crops.
       
      THE SOLUTION:
      Uses General Algebraic Modeling System to Solve this Linear Program
       
      David E Rosenberg
      david.rosenberg@usu.edu
      September 15, 2015
  25   
  26  * 1. DEFINE the SETS
  27  SETS vehicle Type of vehicle to produce /coup, minivan/
  28       res resources /Metal, CircuitBoards, Labor/;
  29   
  30  * 2. DEFINE input data
  31  PARAMETERS
  32     c(vehicle) Objective function coefficients ($ per vehicle)
  33           /coup 6000,
  34          minivan 7000 /
  35   
  36     b(res) Right hand constraint values (per resource)
  37            /Metal 4000000,
  38             CircuitBoards  12000,
  39             Labor  17500/;
  40   
  41  TABLE A(vehicle,res) Left hand side constraint coefficients
  42                   Metal    CircuitBoards   Labor
  43   Coup            1000      4                5
  44   Minivan        2000      3                2.5;
  45   
  46  *PARAMETER UB(plnt) Upper bound on decision variables
  47  * / Eggplant 10000, Tomatoes 700/;
  48  ************
  49  *TABLE A(plnt,res) Left hand side constraint coefficients;
  50  *A("Eggplant", "Water") = 1000;
  51  *A("Eggplant", "Land") = 4;
  52   
  53   
  54  *******
  55   
  56  * 3. DEFINE the variables
  57  VARIABLES X(vehicle) plants planted (Number)
  58            VPROFIT  total profit ($);
  59   
  60  * Non-negativity constraints
  61  POSITIVE VARIABLES X;
  62   
  63  * 4. COMBINE variables and data in equations
  64  EQUATIONS
  65     PROFIT Total profit ($) and objective function value
  66     RES_CONSTRAIN(res) Resource Constraints;
  67  *   LowerLimitDecisionVariables(plnt) Lower limit on decision variable
  68  *   UpperBoundDecisionVariables(plnt) Upper bound on decision variables;
  69   
  70  PROFIT..                 VPROFIT =E= SUM(vehicle, c(vehicle)*X(vehicle));
  71  RES_CONSTRAIN(res) ..    SUM(vehicle, A(vehicle,res)*X(vehicle)) =L= b(res);
  72  *LowerLimitDecisionVariables(plnt)..     X(plnt) =G= 10;
  73  *UpperBoundDecisionVariables(plnt)..    X(plnt) =L= ub(plnt);
  74   
  75  * 5. DEFINE the MODEL from the EQUATIONS
  76  MODEL PLANTING /PROFIT, RES_CONSTRAIN/;
  77  *MODEL AmmonsPLANTING /PROFIT, RES_CONSTRAIN, LowerLimitDecisionVariables, UpperBoundDecisionVariables /;
  78  *Altnerative way to write (include all previously defined equations)
  79  *MODEL PLANTING /ALL/;
  80   
  81  PlANTING.dictfile = 4;
  82  PLANTING.optfile  = 1;
  83  option lp = cplex;
  84   
  85  * 6. SOLVE the MODEL
  86  * Solve the PLANTING model using a Linear Programming Solver (see File=>Options=>Solvers)
  87  *     to maximize VPROFIT
  88  SOLVE PLANTING USING LP MAXIMIZING VPROFIT;
  89   
  90  *SOLVE AmmonsPLANTING USING LP MAXIMIZING VPROFIT;
  91   
  92   
  93   
  94  * 6. CLick File menu => RUN (F9) or Solve icon and examine solution report in .LST file


